{"html":"<h1>Two readers on the same 200 records: Codex and Qwen3.8 27B</h1>\n<p>An experiment for step 09, run on 2026-11-15. Status: done, differences not judged.</p>\n<h2>The question</h2>\n<p>In step 09 a language model reads each record and lists the persons and places in it.\nThe full run used Codex (gpt-6-astra), a paid model on a company's servers.\nCan an open model on our own laptop do the same reading?</p>\n<h2>What stayed the same, and what changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Same:</strong> the 200 pilot records in the same 8 batches, the same instructions (<code>prompt.md</code>), the same answer shape (<code>schema.json</code>), and the same linking rules afterwards (<code>link.py</code>). The prompt text sent to both readers is identical; the fingerprints (SHA-256) in the two runs' records match.</li>\n<li><strong>Changed:</strong> only the reader.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Codex</th>\n<th>Qwen</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>model</td>\n<td>gpt-6-astra, medium effort</td>\n<td>qwen3.8:27b, 4-bit, 18 GB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>where it runs</td>\n<td>the company's servers</td>\n<td>a laptop (Apple M1 Max, 64 GB), through Ollama</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>settings</td>\n<td>defaults</td>\n<td>temperature 0, thinking off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>batches at once</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cost</td>\n<td>$3.16 at list price</td>\n<td>no charge per token</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>text leaves the laptop</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Results</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Codex</th>\n<th>Qwen</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>time for the 8 batches</td>\n<td>about 3 min</td>\n<td>19 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>answers in the required shape</td>\n<td>8 of 8</td>\n<td>8 of 8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>person strings</td>\n<td>919</td>\n<td>710</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>place strings</td>\n<td>726</td>\n<td>709</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>strings that are not in the record (invented)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>person mentions linked to CBDB</td>\n<td>253</td>\n<td>207</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>place mentions linked to TGAZ</td>\n<td>371</td>\n<td>355</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>How far the two agree:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>persons</th>\n<th>places</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the same string in the same record, both readers</td>\n<td>659</td>\n<td>595</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>only Codex</td>\n<td>260</td>\n<td>131</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>only Qwen</td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td>114</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the same id in the same record, both readers</td>\n<td>203</td>\n<td>342</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an id in that record from Codex only</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>21</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>an id in that record from Qwen only</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>11</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The two readers agree closely on the names that get an id.\nMost of the difference is in names that have no id in CBDB or TGAZ anyway.</p>\n<h2>What Qwen missed</h2>\n<p>Of the 260 person strings that only Codex returned:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>97 are full names that are not in CBDB (eunuchs, minor officers, foreigners).</li>\n<li>64 are titles (皇太后, 皇太子, 太宗文皇帝, 樂安王).</li>\n<li>20 are one-character names (祐, 宏, 濬, 紳).</li>\n<li>48 are names that got a CBDB id in Codex's run.</li>\n<li>Long lists are the weak point. In record 115-0034 Qwen missed 29 persons, and in record 139-0044 it missed 17 places.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What Qwen added</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Places that the instructions exclude.</strong> Rule 3 of the prompt says palace buildings, offices and general words are not places. Qwen returned 乾清宮, 午門, 奉天門, 內閣, 禮部, 宮中, 天下 (6 times), 天地, 山林. This is why its place count looks close to Codex's although it found fewer real places.</li>\n<li><strong>Words that are not persons:</strong> 盛暑 and 祁寒 (summer heat and winter cold).</li>\n<li><strong>Two invented strings:</strong> 魏驥 in record 197-0014, and 南京 in a record that does not contain it. The check in <code>link.py</code> (every string must be in the record) caught both and dropped them.</li>\n<li>Some of Qwen's extra strings may be real finds that Codex missed: consort titles among the persons (皇貴妃, 賢妃), and princely houses among the places (代府, 晉府).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What this does not show</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Which reader is right where they differ.</strong> Nobody has judged the 556 differences. The table below lists all of them.</li>\n<li><strong>How either reader compares with a historian.</strong> There is no hand-made answer key for these records.</li>\n<li><strong>Whether thinking mode helps Qwen.</strong> It was off in this trial.</li>\n<li><strong>Other open models, or a larger Qwen.</strong> Only one model was tried, once.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Scale</h2>\n<p>At this speed the whole reign (544 batches) would take about 21 hours on this laptop with Qwen.\nCodex took 2.4 hours.</p>\n<h2>Files</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>extract_local.py</code>: sends the batches to a local model through Ollama.</li>\n<li><code>compare.py</code>: compares two runs string by string.</li>\n<li><code>pilot_qwen/raw/</code>: the 8 answers and a record of each call (model, settings, seconds, tokens).</li>\n<li><code>pilot_qwen/out/compare.json</code>, <code>compare.csv</code>: the counts, and every string found by only one reader.</li>\n</ul>","rows":[[false,false,"001-0001","祐","name","one-character-name",""],[false,false,"001-0001","祐極","name","record-undated",""],[false,false,"003-0042","文貴","name","only-dated-candidate","文貴"],[false,false,"004-0014","宏","name","only-dated-candidate","周宏"],[false,false,"004-0014","忱","name","no-candidate-fits",""],[false,false,"004-0014","澤","name","not-in-cbdb",""],[false,false,"004-0014","盛","name","ambiguous",""],[false,false,"004-0034","母后","title","title-only",""],[false,false,"004-0034","皇太后","title","title-only",""],[false,false,"004-0041","母妃","title","title-only",""],[false,false,"005-0011","皇太后","title","tit